meta {
  name: List what runs on a server
  type: http
  seq: 17
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/organizations/{{organization_id}}/cloud-servers/:cloudServer_id/workloads
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

params:path {
  cloudServer_id: 
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
}

docs {
  The agent's processes, systemd services and timers, and containers, as workloads: processes attached to the service or container the agent says they run under (`attributed` is `cgroup`), or, where it did not say, grouped by command and attached to the service they are named after (`attributed` is `name`); timers carry `last_run_at` and `next_run_at`. Each with memory, CPU where known, restarts in the last 24 hours (`restarts_24h`) and the last 7 days (`restarts_7d`; when `restarts_7d_capped` is true the count reached the 100 InfraNest keeps, so read it as "at least"), a 7-day hourly memory `trend` (oldest first, null where nothing was sampled) and `observations`: `failed`, `restart_loop`, `memory_leak` (grows and resets on restart) or `no_heartbeat` (a scheduled job of yours that no heartbeat monitor watches), each with a `certainty`. Problems come first. Each row also says what it is, from InfraNest's workload catalogue: `catalogue` is the matched entry (`id`, the software's `name`, its `group`, its homepage `url`, and `generic` when it names a family such as `*-worker` rather than one product) or null when the name is not recognised — a timer is matched by the job it starts (`apt-daily.timer` is APT); `app` is the app a generic runtime runs, read from its command line (`node /srv/shop/server.js` is `shop`), or null; `group` is where it is listed (`web`, `app`, `database`, `cache`, `queue`, `search`, `storage`, `monitoring`, `security`, `network`, `mail`, `dns`, `backup`, `containers`, `panel`, `ci`, `system`, `scheduled` for a timer, `installed` for a service named by its package, `other` when not recognised); `noise` is true for the operating system's own plumbing, and for a timer the catalogue knows as part of the system — never for a unit that failed, which is always listed. `finished` is true for a service that ran once and is done — nothing of it runs and it ended without failing (`exited`, or `inactive`), like the jobs a server runs at boot; it then has `noise` true too, unless you named it, a rule watches it, or it is a stopped program that gives the server a role (a database, a web server). A container is recognised by its main process, which your agent reads from the container's cgroup (`main_process`, and `main_command` when your agent sends command lines), and by its largest reported process with an older agent; `named` is true when you saved a name for it, which wins. `ports` lists what Docker publishes for a container (`protocol`, `host_ip`, `host_port`, `container_port`), read from its `docker-proxy` processes. `package` is the Debian package a row runs from, as your agent read it from the server's package database (`name`, `version`, `description` — the package's own one-line summary — `source`, `dpkg`, and the package's `priority` and `section` as Debian sets them, null from an agent before 0.19), or null when your agent did not say; each process carries its own `package` too. For a service or timer with nothing running, `package` is the package that installed its unit file. `packaged` is true for a service or timer the catalogue does not know that is named and described by that package instead: listed under `installed`, or under `system` with `noise` true when the package's priority (`required`, `important` or `standard`) makes it part of the operating system. `local` is true for a unit written on the server itself — its file is in `/etc/systemd/system` and no package installed it — which stays yours to name. On Windows, every service your agent lists is a `service` row under its service name (`MSSQLSERVER`, `W3SVC`), with its display name as `description`: recognised by its service name (`W3SVC` is IIS), and with the process it runs in among its `processes` when that process runs it alone; Windows' own services have `noise` true. `hosts` lists the services a process row's processes run, by display name, and each process its own `services` — how one `svchost.exe` is told from another. `merged_keys` lists the other command lines folded into a process row of the same program. `evidence` lists what is known about it, each with a `key` and `params`: `identical` (`n` identical processes), `in_container` (`id`), `port` (its usual `port` is listening, with `scope` `local`, `specific` or `all`), `monitors` (`n` monitors check it), `serves` (a `name` pointing at the server and how many `more`, only when you may read domains) and `no_args` (a runtime whose command line was not reported, so the app it runs is unknown) and `published` (a container's host `port`, the `container_port` it goes to, and `scope` `local`, `specific` or `all`). `first_seen_at` is when it was first sampled, within the 15 days of samples kept; `new_this_week` is true when that was in the last 7 days and the server was already sampled before. `normal` is the range its memory keeps over the week (`low` and `high`, the 10th and 90th percentile hours) and the latest hour's `state` against it (`within`, `above` or `below`), or null with less than two days of samples. `platform` says what your agent reads on the server’s system, in the same shape as on the agent snapshot: where `reads.containers`, `reads.process_args` or `reads.cron` is false, there are no containers, command lines or cron jobs because they are not read there, not because there are none. It is null until your agent has reported its system. What you said about a workload: `identity` is the program it runs as your workspace's names are kept under (a unit's name — a timer's is the job it starts, so a timer and its service share one — a process's executable or a container's main program, normalised), or null; `custom` is the name and `group` your workspace gave that program (`id`, `name`, `group`, `hidden` when you hid it as noise — it then has `noise` true — and `shared` when you chose to share that name with InfraNest so it can recognise the program for everyone), or null; `expected` says who marked it expected this week on this server and when (`by`, `at`), or null; `watch` is how a rule watching it alone knows it (a service's unit, a container's main program and lowest published port), or null for a process or a timer; `alert` is that rule (`rule_id`, `is_active`), or null. `jobs` lists everything that runs on a schedule — cron jobs (your agent reads `/etc/crontab`, `/etc/cron.d` and the run-parts folders from 0.20; your users' own crontabs are readable by root alone and are not listed) and systemd timers — each with a `key` (`cron:<file>:<line>`, or `timer:<unit>`), `source` (`cron` or `timer`), `name` (the job name the line reports under, else the name your workspace gave the program, else the catalogue's name, else the program), `catalogue` (`id`, `name`) or null, `program`, `file`, `line`, `folder` (`hourly`, `daily`, `weekly` or `monthly` for a run-parts script), `schedule` as written, `when` (its shape: `type` `minutes` with `n`, `hourly` with `minutes`, `daily`, `weekly` with `days` (0 is Sunday) or `monthly` with `days`, each with `times` in the server's own time; `reboot`; `anacron` with its `period`; `timer` with its `cadence` in seconds; `cron` with the `expr` when none of these says it), `timezone` the schedule is read in, `run_as`, `command` (only when your agent sends command lines, else null), `job` (the `--job` name when the line runs through `infranest-agent run`), `last_run` (`at`, `outcome` `worked` or `failed`, `exit_code`, `duration_ms`) or null — a cron job's last run is only known from a job that reports through the agent, which `last_recorded` says — `next_run_at` (computed from the schedule in its time zone; null for `@reboot` and for a folder anacron runs), `cadence_seconds`, `watched` (the heartbeat monitor watching it: `id`, `name`, `status`, `last_ping_at`, and `waiting` until its first check-in) or null, `noise` (the operating system's housekeeping, or a program you hid), `identity` and `custom` as on a workload row — a cron job's `identity` is its program without its folder or a script's extension (`backup.sh` is `backup`), so naming it names it on every server of your workspace — and `overlaps`: a maintenance window naming this server (`kind` `window`, with `id`, `name` and `one_time`) or a scheduled reboot (`kind` `reboot`) that starts while the job's next run would be running, within a day, with `starts_at`, `ends_at`, the run it cuts into (`job_at`) and the first whole hour after that run (`clear_at`). `job_reports` is whether your agent has its folder for job reports (null from an agent before 0.20). A service also carries `errors_trend`, its errors per hour from the server's system log over the same 7 days, oldest first (null where your agent never named it; 0 in an hour it counted without it), and `journal`, its last lines from the system log for the failure it is in now or a restart loop in the last day — `event` (`failed`, `oom_kill` or `restart_loop`), `at`, `lines` (each with `at`, `priority` 0–7 and `message`, with anything shaped like a password, token or key masked), `compressed` (lines stored compressed that your agent does not read), `truncated`, `lines_withheld` (`login` when the service is a login service such as sshd: its lines are records of who logged in, so your agent sends none and `lines` is empty; null otherwise), `id`, `lines_hidden` (true when `lines` was left out because you cannot manage servers — `cloud.write`, on your role and on your token) and `lines_removed` (who removed the lines and when, `by` and `at`, or null) — or null. `journal` at the top says whether your agent reads the system log: `readable`, `reason` (`ok`, `off`, `no_permission`, `no_journal` or `unknown_format`) and `at` (when that last changed), or null before agent 0.22 and off Linux. Your agent reads it only once you add its user to the `systemd-journal` group, and keeps lines 30 days. Needs the server's agent.

  Scope: `cloud.read`
}
